nd of like how there are  literary devices that help make speech  or text a lot more effective in terms of  communication there are compositional  devices that do the same as well just in  musical terms so in getting to the  arrangement I know that I'm going to be  using the Sonata Allegro form I'll have  two themes and I'll of course reference  the happy birthday melody I think the  first order of business is to come up  with the two themes and for that I'm  going to start off with happy birthday  in the key of D minor  from that I'm going to focus on just the  first two pitches because when you pay  attention to what Beethoven does for  example in his fifth symphony that we  all know the rest of the entire symphony  movement can always be traced back to  those opening pitches which is only two  pitches of course the repeated pitch and  then a pitch a third below but really  everything stems from that so in similar  fashion I'm choosing the first two  pitches from happy birthday in D minor  and instead of having a single line I  think it'll be more interesting in  thirds this is what you call a motive or  a motif basically core material from  which a bunch of other phrases other  sections get built from so in order to  build from somewhere it's great to start  with something very small and basic like  two pitches I purposely made the  direction of these gestures opposite  from one another it's almost like a  question and an answer in a very micro  form and then there is a longer gesture  so it's all three components make up a  short short long phrase now on to the  second theme traditionally the key of  the second theme is related somehow to  that of the first for example if we're  in a major key the second theme will  most likely be in the dominant key which  is the five so C major to say G major in  minor keys most often you'll find the  second theme in the relative major so  major three or also five so with that in  mind my second theme will be in F major  the relative major key to D minor and  I'm going to use this part or happy  birthday dear so and so from the  original happy birthday melody as a  melodic material and I want it to be in  contrast to the first theme  now that I have the main pillars for my  Sonata I need to figure out ways to  connect the dots and fill in the blanks  so here I have both volumes of the  Beethoven sonatas volume 1 volume 2 this  happened when I got really frustrated  you know I'm just kidding I'm not sure  why this so what I'm going to do now is  I'm going to record as many of these  phrases as I can possibly manage right  now and from that I'm going to compile a  list of compositional characteristics  that I see of Beethoven's music  especially in his piano sonatas repeated  chords  more courts wide broken chords  [Music]  Unison's or two lines spaced an octave  apart playing the same exact line hand  crossings specifically this type  broken octaves staggered Octus contrast  a lot of contrast diminished chords  which you find everywhere both in chord  form and arpeggiated form  long trills  [Music]  imitative layers  themes with horn calls and horns in  general  [Music]  enter lines extremely warm and beautiful  melodies  [Music]  a lot of times you'll find scales and  arpeggios just in their straight form  for an entire phrase either as a single  note or in octaves and a lot of times I  think that's kind of hard to pull off  but Beethoven makes it sound pretty  emphatic and epic this is probably the  most intense descending minor scale in  all of piano repertoire  [Music]  the clam Ettore moments which are also  accompanied by rests and bouts of  silence  [Music]  call-and-response  repeated peddle points  outer petal lines so this is sort of the  opposite of a petal point you'll find a  note or an octave surrounding certain  chords that are moving that stays the  same  [Music]  turns  sequences repeated pairs hard  oh okay I think that's enough for today  now I'm focusing a lot on these two  pitches a and b-flat and specifically  the semitone relationship another thing  I want to mention is the way that I  played it because that's quite important  say that I played it with a lot more  pedal with a gentler fuzzier type of  sound it might sound like this  and now again the way that I played it  [Music]  [Applause]  so as you can hear there is a huge  difference even though I'm playing the  exact same pitches with the exact same  rhythm and in this particular case I  think a term that comes in handy is non  legato which is kind of halfway between  something that is staccato short and dry  and something that is legato long fluid  connected of course there are many other  factors and many other things that are  involved in the way you play that will  make your playing sound more or less  Beethoven but I think that depends on  the piece now after a number of drafts  and revisions here is happy birthday in  the style of Ludwig van Beethoven in  honor of his 250th celebration  you  [Music]  and making additional footage from this  project available to my patrons on  patreon such as a more in-depth analysis  of a Beethoven piano sonata more on how  I came up with my own arrangement and  extended versions of some of the  examples shown in the video as always  thank you so much for watching and I'll  see you in the next video I'm not really  sure if this is possible why do I feel  stressed I feel stressed okay

